; One-man walking gun platform. See mech.gd: it is a Tank that happens to have
; legs, so climbing in, handing it over the wire, the armour rules and both
; weapons are the tank's and are not restated here.
;
; Deliberately of its period. Nothing here is a composite panel or a smooth
; moulded shell: it is rolled and cast steel, bolted at the seams, a vision slit
; rather than a screen, a stowage box on the back and an exhaust stack either
; side of the hull. The proportions are a field gun's -- squat, wide-footed and
; nose-heavy -- rather than an athlete's.
;
; Very little of it is a box, and that is the whole trick to it looking like a
; machine rather than a stack of crates. Limbs are capsules with tapered sleeves
; over them, so they have a round section and a thickness that changes along
; their length. Joints are balls in collars, which is both what a joint looks
; like and what lets one rotate without a corner passing through its own
; housing. The hull is a pair of many-sided cast drums rather than a slab. Armour
; is wedges -- prisms -- laid on at an angle, because sloped plate is what the
; period actually did and a flat face is what reads as blocky. Boxes are left
; only where the real thing would be one: the stowage bin and the ammunition
; drum.
;
; The hierarchy is what the gait needs. Each leg is Leg -> Knee -> Ankle, three
; nested pivots with the geometry hung off them, so mech.gd swings a whole leg
; by turning one node and the shin and foot come with it. The torso is called
; Turret because that is the node tank.gd traverses; here it is the whole upper
; body, and the arms pitch inside it as one under Gun.
;
; Three rotations do all the work below, written out rather than left as magic:
;   (1,0,0, 0,1,0, 0,0,1)    upright
;   (1,0,0, 0,0,-1, 0,1,0)   lay the long axis forward along -Z
;   (0,1,0, -1,0,0, 0,0,1)   lay the long axis across the machine, along X
